<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Monzón</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Monzón is a town in the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huesca">Huesca</a>, Aragon, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cinca">Cinca</a>. Its castle, taken from the Muslims in 1089, later belonged to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knights-templar">Knights Templar</a>, who brought up the young King James I there. The town often hosted the general Corts of the Crown of Aragon, joint assemblies of Aragon, Catalonia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a>, from the 13th to the 17th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/monzon.jpg" alt="Monzón" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Francisco Martinez Arias from Monzon, España · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Castillo_de_Monzon_(15530066601).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
